WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.539 --> 00:00:03.320
Jerry Springer has died at the age of 79.

00:00:03.320 --> 00:00:07.650
But what do we know about his death — and
the incredible, strange, and controversial

00:00:07.650 --> 00:00:08.650
life he led?

00:00:08.650 --> 00:00:12.469
The famous talk show host and mastermind behind
The Jerry Springer Show was diagnosed with

00:00:12.469 --> 00:00:16.309
cancer a few months ago, but his condition
worsened earlier this week.

00:00:16.309 --> 00:00:19.420
He was in his home, located in the Chicago
area, at the time of his passing.

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.939
A family spokesperson released a statement
on behalf of the talk show host's family,

00:00:23.939 --> 00:00:25.939
noting he was loved by lots of people.

00:00:25.939 --> 00:00:26.939
It read:

00:00:26.939 --> 00:00:30.080
"Jerry's ability to connect with people was
at the heart of his success in everything

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.080
he tried.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.850
[...] He's irreplaceable and his loss hurts
immensely, but memories of his intellect,

00:00:34.850 --> 00:00:37.040
heart, and humor will live on."

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:39.660
Of course, Springer leaves behind an impressive
legacy.

00:00:39.660 --> 00:00:43.940
While he was primarily associated with television,
he had a long career that saw him take on

00:00:43.940 --> 00:00:47.809
a variety of jobs, ranging from political
pursuits to movie roles.

00:00:47.809 --> 00:00:51.870
Prior to becoming one of the most recognizable
faces on television, Jerry Springer was a

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:54.870
politician and a supporter of the Democratic
Party.

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:59.180
After serving as Robert F. Kennedy's political
campaign adviser, he unsuccessfully ran for

00:00:59.180 --> 00:01:00.600
Congress in 1970.

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:04.720
However, he was elected to Cincinnati City
Council the following year and became the

00:01:04.720 --> 00:01:07.020
mayor of the city in 1977.

00:01:07.020 --> 00:01:10.870
That said, he was only allowed to hold the
position of mayor for a year.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:15.040
Most people associate Springer with his talk
show, which he hosted for 27 years.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:19.700
The Jerry Springer Show rose to prominence
in the 1990s and attracted a lot of controversy

00:01:19.700 --> 00:01:21.409
due to the topics it covered.

00:01:21.409 --> 00:01:25.740
The series originally started out as a humble
political and news commentary show, but it

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:31.680
truly took off after it started covering stories
about… this kind of stuff.

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:32.680
*slap*

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:37.259
Critics often accused the show of being sensationalist
and exploitative, but millions of viewers

00:01:37.259 --> 00:01:39.190
were captivated by its content.

00:01:39.190 --> 00:01:42.620
It wasn't uncommon for the guests to start
fights with each other on The Jerry Springer

00:01:42.620 --> 00:01:47.219
Show, but the drama helped the series become
a ratings smash in the United States.

00:01:47.219 --> 00:01:51.020
Springer's controversial juggernaut even drew
more viewers than The Oprah Winfrey Show in

00:01:51.020 --> 00:01:54.040
the late '90s, which was no small feat at
the time.

00:01:54.040 --> 00:01:57.860
Springer's success turned him into a household
name, so it was only a matter of time until

00:01:57.860 --> 00:01:59.240
he made the leap to acting.

00:01:59.240 --> 00:02:03.189
While his political career only saw him work
at the local level, he did get to play the

00:02:03.189 --> 00:02:06.840
president of the United States in Dolph Lundgren's
action-thriller The Defender.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.420
For the most part, though, Springer was either
cast as himself or as characters with similar

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.940
interests to his own.

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:17.080
His most famous movie role is Ringmaster,
in which he portrays a talk show host named

00:02:17.080 --> 00:02:18.110
Jerry Farrelly.

00:02:18.110 --> 00:02:22.230
While the movie didn't set the box office
on fire, it did showcase Springer's abilities

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:26.129
as an actor and proved that he was more than
happy to satirize himself.

00:02:26.129 --> 00:02:31.150
In 1999, he had a brief cameo in Austin Powers:
The Spy Who Shagged Me, in which he played

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:33.140
himself and interviewed Dr. Evil and his son.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.140
"Okay, everything's okay.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:35.140
Get this jerk out of here."

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:40.980
"I'll give you [bleep], you piece of [bleep]!"

00:02:40.980 --> 00:02:45.230
Springer also guest starred on several TV
shows, from The X-Files to The Simpsons.

00:02:45.230 --> 00:02:49.319
His final acting role was on The House Arrest
Rooneys, a comedy about a family that gets

00:02:49.319 --> 00:02:51.349
caught up in an insurance fraud scam.

00:02:51.349 --> 00:02:55.590
Meanwhile, his last general television appearance
was on The Masked Singer last year.

00:02:55.590 --> 00:03:00.260
No doubt, Springer leaves behind a legacy
as unusual as the show that made him famous.

